data: {"id":"chatcmpl-a32676fd-d91c-4bd5-a767-d160bc63a367","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790745124,"model":"capitoline-judge-sol6","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"**Use EC-X if the backups are mostly written once and then left unchanged. Use plain RF2 if the backup software frequently rewrites existing data or restore performance is the priority.** Six nodes and RF2 alone do not settle the choice.\n\nRF2 keeps two copies: about **2 TB of raw capacity per 1 TB of logical data**, before compression or deduplication. For data encoded in a 4-data + 1-parity EC-X stripe, the figure is about **1.25 TB raw per 1 TB logical**. Actual savings depend on eligible data, stripe placement, and how much data is changed or deleted.\n\nEC-X saves capacity at the cost of CPU, network traffic, and background encoding work. Incoming data initially uses RF2 space, so savings take time and require capacity headroom. In-place synthetic-full merges, reverse incrementals, and other frequent overwrites create extra work and can erode the savings. After a failure, EC-X must reconstruct missing data from surviving fragments, which can make degraded reads and rebuilds more demanding.\n\nPlain RF2 costs more capacity but offers simpler recovery and more predictable performance under heavy rewrite or restore workloads. **EC-X does not provide RF3 protection**; plan for one-node failure and reserve rebuild headroom either way. Check your AOS version’s EC-X behavior and test with your backup software’s actual write and restore patterns."},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-a32676fd-d91c-4bd5-a767-d160bc63a367","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790745125,"model":"capitoline-judge-sol6","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":""},"finish_reason":null}],"capitoline":{"stage":"synthesis","done":1,"total":1}}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-a32676fd-d91c-4bd5-a767-d160bc63a367","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790745125,"model":"capitoline-judge-sol6","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":45243,"completion_tokens":4667,"total_tokens":49910},"capitoline":{"provider":"capitoline","ignored":[],"council":{"deliberationId":"dfc1ba07-0cd0-424b-8199-1fb80d52da1e","strategyVersion":2,"shape":"fast","members":[{"family":"anthropic","model":"claude-opus","label":"Response C","answer":"**Short answer:** usually EC-X. Backup data is the workload Nutanix designed it for: written once, rarely read, rarely overwritten. The choice depends on how your backup software writes.\n\n**What EC-X gives you.** On six nodes at RF2, AOS typically uses a 4+1 stripe, because one node's worth of capacity is held back for rebuilds. That stores data at about 1.25× raw overhead instead of RF2's 2×, so usable capacity for cold data rises by roughly 60%. Fault tolerance is the same as RF2: it survives one node or disk failure.\n\n**What EC-X costs:**\n- **Delay:** Encoding runs as a post-process on write-cold data. New writes land as RF2 and are converted later, so savings arrive after a delay. The exact threshold depends on your AOS version.\n- **Overwrites:** Overwriting encoded data leaves holes in stripes that need cleanup, which adds I/O, CPU and garbage. EC-X is a poor fit for reverse-incremental chains, frequent synthetic-full merges done in place, or aggressive retention pruning that rewrites blocks.\n- **Failures:** Rebuilds are slower and use more CPU and network, because data is reconstructed from parity. Reads while degraded also need reconstruction.\n- **Tuning:** Stripe width and savings change as nodes are added or removed.\n\n**What plain RF2 gives you:** simpler behaviour, faster rebuilds, and predictable performance under churn, at double the raw capacity.\n\n**Recommendation:** Use EC-X with compression if the backup chain is append-mostly, such as forward incrementals with periodic active fulls. Stay on RF2 if the software constantly rewrites existing backup files. Check Prism's EC savings after a retention cycle to confirm.","fellBackFrom":["claude-fable (rate_limited)"]},{"family":"openai","model":"codex-gpt-6-astra","label":"Response B","answer":"**Either can be appropriate; six nodes and RF2 alone do not determine the answer.** Choose based on the backup workload, restore requirements, capacity pressure, and the EC-X behavior supported by your AOS version.\n\nEC-X is attractive for large, long-lived backup files that become cold and change little. RF2 stores two copies, consuming roughly **2 TB raw per 1 TB logical**, before compression or deduplication. A **4-data + 1-parity** EC stripe consumes about **1.25 TB raw per 1 TB logical**: approximately 37.5% less space than RF2. Actual savings depend on stripe width, eligible data, and other data-reduction features.\n\nThose savings cost CPU, network traffic, and background work to encode data. Degraded reads and reconstruction require reading surviving stripe fragments and computing missing data. Overwrites, synthetic-full merges, and retention churn can increase work and reduce savings. With post-process EC-X, incoming data initially consumes RF2 space, so encoding requires time and capacity headroom.\n\nPlain RF2 sacrifices capacity efficiency for simpler recovery and more predictable performance, particularly with frequent rewrites or demanding restore-time targets.\n\nBoth provide protection against one node failure when correctly placed; EC-X does **not** turn RF2 into RF3. Reserve rebuild headroom with either choice. Validate supported stripe placement and degraded operation for your version, then benchmark representative backup, merge, restore, and failure scenarios."},{"family":"google","model":"antigravity-gemini-pro","label":"Response A","answer":"For storing backup data on a six-node Nutanix RF2 cluster, the backup container should generally use **Erasure Coding (EC-X)**, but the optimal choice depends heavily on your backup software's specific I/O patterns.\n\n**What the answer depends on:**\n*   **Write Patterns:** EC-X is ideal for Write-Once, Read-Rarely data, which perfectly describes traditional full and incremental backup streams. However, if your backup application frequently overwrites existing blocks (e.g., active reverse incrementals or in-place synthetic fulls), EC-X will introduce a severe read-modify-write performance penalty.\n*   **Resource Availability:** EC-X trades compute for capacity. If your cluster is highly CPU-constrained, the background parity calculations could theoretically impact performance, though backup targets typically prioritize capacity over compute. \n\n**What each choice costs:**\n*   **Plain RF2:**\n    *   *Cost:* High storage expense. RF2 carries a 100% capacity overhead (1TB of data consumes 2TB of raw space).\n    *   *Benefit:* Excellent random overwrite performance, minimal CPU/memory overhead, and faster data rebuild times during a node failure.\n*   **EC-X (with RF2):**\n    *   *Cost:* Increased CPU utilization for post-process Curator scans (which calculate the parity) and steep latency penalties for data overwrites.\n    *   *Benefit:* Massive storage savings. On a six-node cluster, EC-X can use a 4:1 stripe, reducing the capacity overhead from 100% down to roughly 25%. This drastically lowers the hardware cost for cold data.\n"},{"family":"open-weights","model":"antigravity-gpt-oss","label":"Response D","answer":"**Short answer:**  \nUse erasure‑coding (EC‑X) for the backup container **if you need to save storage space and can tolerate a modest CPU/network overhead and slightly slower restores**; otherwise stick with plain RF2 when you need the simplest, fastest access and you have enough capacity.\n\n**What the decision depends on**\n\n| Factor | RF2 (2‑way replication) | EC‑X (erasure coding) |\n|--------|------------------------|-----------------------|\n| **Storage overhead** | 2 × (100 % extra) – every block is stored twice | Typically 1.5 × – 2 × (e.g., EC‑4 → 1.5×, EC‑6 → ~1.67×) |\n| **CPU / network cost** | Minimal – just copy the block | Encoding/decoding uses CVM CPU and extra inter‑node traffic; each write incurs extra parity calculations |\n| **Write latency** | Low – just two copies | Slightly higher because of encoding and distributing parity shards |\n| **Read latency / availability** | Immediate – any copy can serve reads | Reads may need to reconstruct if a shard is missing; otherwise similar to RF2 |\n| **Rebuild time after a node loss** | Fast – copy from the remaining replica | Slower – must recompute parity from the surviving shards; more network traffic |\n| **Failure tolerance** | Tolerates loss of **one** node (or one replica) | Same fault tolerance (depends on X), but requires enough surviving shards to rebuild |\n\n**Cost considerations**\n\n- **Capacity:** With a six‑node cluster, RF2 consumes roughly 50 % of raw capacity for backups. EC‑X can shrink that to 33‑40 % of raw capacity, freeing space for other workloads.\n- **Performance:** If your backup window is generous and restores are not latency‑critical, the extra CPU and network load of EC‑X is acceptable. If you need the fastest possible restores or have limited CVM CPU headroom, RF2 is safer.\n- **Complexity:** EC‑X adds configuration complexity (choosing X, monitoring parity health). RF2 is “set‑and‑forget”.\n\n**Bottom line:** Choose **EC‑X** when storage efficiency is a priority and you can absorb the modest performance hit; choose **RF2** when you favor simplicity, maximum read speed, and have spare capacity.\n"}],"lost":[],"rankings":[],"aggregate":[],"judge":{"model":"codex-gpt-6-sol","blind":true},"calls":5}}}

